    "recommendations": "Reducing direct vegetation contact with the structure and creating greater separation between brush, shrubs, and building surfaces could meaningfully improve defensible space and reduce fuel continuity in the immediate structure zone. Where tree clusters are present, thinning to increase spacing between canopies and removing lower limbs may reduce vertical fuel continuity and support suppression operations. Managing scattered shrubs and mixed vegetation cover across the property, particularly where it connects fuel from open terrain toward the structure, would help interrupt fuel pathways. Vegetation growing adjacent to the access route should be trimmed back to maintain clearance along both sides, which would support firefighter access and improve navigation during suppression operations. Expanding and maintaining the irrigated landscape zone around the structure may provide a useful buffer that reduces fuel continuity near improvements. Continued long-term vegetation management across the property, including periodic reassessment of regrowth near the structure perimeter, could improve overall wildfire resilience."
